WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.667 --> 00:00:03.200
♪♪

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:05.667
-All right,
so I mimed Ron DeSantis

00:00:05.667 --> 00:00:08.834
eating pudding with two fingers.

00:00:08.834 --> 00:00:13.434
He allegedly eats pudding
with three fingers.

00:00:13.434 --> 00:00:15.534
[ Light laughter ]

00:00:15.534 --> 00:00:17.367
Two fingers for yogurt.

00:00:17.367 --> 00:00:19.167
[ Laughter ]

00:00:21.701 --> 00:00:26.267
Graveyards are attached to ch--

00:00:26.267 --> 00:00:29.100
I've been conflating
graveyards and cemeteries,

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:30.167
and there's a difference.

00:00:30.167 --> 00:00:32.601
Graveyards are attached
to churches.

00:00:32.601 --> 00:00:35.567
Cemeteries are attached
to highways.

00:00:35.567 --> 00:00:37.734
[ Laughter ]

00:00:37.734 --> 00:00:40.000
I've always thought
there was something funny --

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:41.701
when you see an old church

00:00:41.701 --> 00:00:45.267
and there's just one of those
tiny, little graveyards

00:00:45.267 --> 00:00:48.100
that, at some point in 1740,
someone said,

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:50.133
"I think that
will be big enough."

00:00:50.133 --> 00:00:52.300
[ Laughter ]

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:54.634
"I think that will be big enough
for everybody forever."

00:00:54.634 --> 00:00:57.467
[ Laughter ]

00:00:57.467 --> 00:00:59.567
[ Chuckling ]
"Unless something bad happens."

00:00:59.567 --> 00:01:01.968
Oh, Cars for Kids.

00:01:01.968 --> 00:01:05.167
We all know
the Cars for Kids commercial.

00:01:05.167 --> 00:01:06.567
It's got that catchy song --

00:01:06.567 --> 00:01:08.467
you know, herpes catchy,
but catchy.

00:01:08.467 --> 00:01:11.200
And...
[ Laughter ]

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:14.167
I believed
it to be a regional ad.

00:01:14.167 --> 00:01:16.934
I guess it's gone national now.

00:01:16.934 --> 00:01:23.467
And I always said, if we didn't
stop them when we had a chance,

00:01:23.467 --> 00:01:25.033
this would happen.

00:01:26.434 --> 00:01:27.634
Um.

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:31.033
Got a chance to do
my Rupert Murdoch impression

00:01:31.033 --> 00:01:34.100
a few times this week...

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:37.534
with our now pretty famous
catchphrase,

00:01:37.534 --> 00:01:39.434
[Australian accent]
"Eat bugs, ya [bleep]!"

00:01:39.434 --> 00:01:40.667
[ Laughter ]

00:01:40.667 --> 00:01:42.033
[ Normal voice ]
Really unfortunate things

00:01:42.033 --> 00:01:43.601
have been happening.

00:01:43.601 --> 00:01:46.734
The closed captioning,
in parentheses,

00:01:46.734 --> 00:01:49.734
has been saying
"Austrian accent."

00:01:49.734 --> 00:01:52.400
[ Laughter ]

00:01:53.767 --> 00:01:57.100
Which must be very jarring
for those who aren't hearing it,

00:01:57.100 --> 00:01:59.267
who wonder why I'm giving
an Austrian accent

00:01:59.267 --> 00:02:02.067
to Rupert Murdoch.

00:02:02.067 --> 00:02:04.133
First of all,
it wouldn't sound like that.

00:02:04.133 --> 00:02:09.567
I mean, if he'd been Austrian,
like -- I don't know --

00:02:09.567 --> 00:02:13.167
Just pick an...
Austrian name out of a hat --

00:02:13.167 --> 00:02:15.100
Christoph Waltz.

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:17.634
[ Laughter ]

00:02:17.634 --> 00:02:18.801
So it would sound like...

00:02:18.801 --> 00:02:20.734
[Austrian accent]
"Eat bugs, Mr. Bond!"

00:02:20.734 --> 00:02:23.033
[ Normal voice ]
So different.

00:02:23.033 --> 00:02:25.367
Okay. So, we showed a clip
of Tucker Carlson

00:02:25.367 --> 00:02:27.100
discussing testicle tanning.

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:28.567
And none of these
are my errors --

00:02:28.567 --> 00:02:29.868
they are Tucker's.

00:02:29.868 --> 00:02:31.968
But he's in a tough spot
right now.

00:02:31.968 --> 00:02:34.434
He doesn't have a show
to address his mistakes.

00:02:34.434 --> 00:02:36.934
So this is a situation where --
next man up,

00:02:36.934 --> 00:02:40.300
I'm going to jump in the fray
here, do him a solid.

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:44.901
And testicle tanning
is a really bad idea.

00:02:44.901 --> 00:02:47.100
If you want to do it at all,
which you probably shouldn't,

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:49.200
you're going to want
to do scrotum tanning

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:53.467
because...
the testicles are inside.

00:02:53.467 --> 00:02:55.601
There's no --
[ Laughter ]

00:02:55.601 --> 00:02:59.801
The sun would have to be so hot
to get through the --

00:02:59.801 --> 00:03:03.834
again, to use a Shoemaker term,
scrotal wall.

00:03:03.834 --> 00:03:05.868
[ Laughter ]

00:03:08.167 --> 00:03:10.434
Or you'd do a whole --
you'd take them out,

00:03:10.434 --> 00:03:13.834
and then you've got to put them
back in once you toast them up.

00:03:13.834 --> 00:03:15.634
And that's,
to use a medical term,

00:03:15.634 --> 00:03:17.267
like, just a whole thing.

00:03:17.267 --> 00:03:19.033
[ Laughter ]

00:03:20.367 --> 00:03:23.901
I said "a-lleg-ed."
It's "alle-ged."

00:03:23.901 --> 00:03:26.701
Or at least
that's what they claimed.

00:03:26.701 --> 00:03:28.667
[ Laughter ]

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:31.968
Oh.

00:03:31.968 --> 00:03:35.434
I said zoologists blew it
with the spelling of "zoologist"

00:03:35.434 --> 00:03:36.934
because they just
should have put --

00:03:36.934 --> 00:03:40.400
if there was ever a time
to put a three O's in a row,

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:41.567
that would be it, right?

00:03:41.567 --> 00:03:44.100
You got the "zoo" part
and you got the "ology."

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:45.968
Just don't shorten it.

00:03:45.968 --> 00:03:49.968
Be the one word with three O's.

00:03:49.968 --> 00:03:55.033
Well...there is already a word
with three O's in a row --

00:03:55.033 --> 00:03:56.968
pardon my pronunciation --

00:03:56.968 --> 00:04:01.033
"hysterosalpingo-oophorectomy,"

00:04:01.033 --> 00:04:05.100
which is a word
that means "hat."

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:06.901
[ Laughter ]

00:04:08.901 --> 00:04:10.100
So, you guys, it is --

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:12.334
I've been giving you
some false information.

00:04:12.334 --> 00:04:15.200
It is a little worse
to look at an eclipse,

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:18.734
because what happens is, when
the sun goes behind the moon,

00:04:18.734 --> 00:04:23.667
the eye dilates to adjust
for the lack of light.

00:04:23.667 --> 00:04:26.767
And then, so when the sun
pokes back out again,

00:04:26.767 --> 00:04:28.100
your eyes aren't ready for it.

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:31.968
And so that is
when you get blasted there.

00:04:31.968 --> 00:04:33.934
That is why it's worse
for you to look.

00:04:33.934 --> 00:04:35.434
The eclipse isn't
the bad moment --

00:04:35.434 --> 00:04:37.400
it's when the sun
starts coming back out.

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:38.834
Your eyes aren't ready for it.

00:04:38.834 --> 00:04:42.067
Interesting fact, though --
that's the only sunlight.

00:04:42.067 --> 00:04:43.634
That moment
where it first comes out,

00:04:43.634 --> 00:04:46.434
that's the only sunlight
hot enough

00:04:46.434 --> 00:04:48.334
to permeate the scrotal wall.

00:04:48.334 --> 00:04:49.934
[ Laughter ]

00:04:51.601 --> 00:04:55.234
We talked about
how Trump wanted a --

00:04:55.234 --> 00:04:56.601
wanted the mug shot
because he thought

00:04:56.601 --> 00:04:57.934
it would be a cool thing.

00:04:57.934 --> 00:05:01.667
And then we said, "Hey,
don't make this one of those" --

00:05:01.667 --> 00:05:03.567
You know,
like the Johnny Cash mug shot

00:05:03.567 --> 00:05:04.868
where, all of a sudden,
it's on a poster

00:05:04.868 --> 00:05:06.868
and people are hanging it
in their dorm rooms,

00:05:06.868 --> 00:05:08.601
because nobody wants to,
you know, go in,

00:05:08.601 --> 00:05:10.267
and you flip on a lava lamp,

00:05:10.267 --> 00:05:14.767
and then all of a sudden, you
see a Trump poster on the wall.

00:05:14.767 --> 00:05:18.968
Well, some lava lamp owners gave
us the news that you never --

00:05:18.968 --> 00:05:21.167
when you flip on a lava lamp,
it's not light.

00:05:21.167 --> 00:05:24.200
It takes like 45 minutes
to get going.

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:25.801
So that was a bad example
of a lamp.

00:05:25.801 --> 00:05:27.367
It's why you've never seen --
there's never been

00:05:27.367 --> 00:05:29.367
that cinematic scene in a movie

00:05:29.367 --> 00:05:31.200
where someone walks
into a dark room,

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:33.300
and then, like,
a lamp gets flipped on

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:35.033
to reveal someone
waiting for them.

00:05:35.033 --> 00:05:36.501
It's never a lava lamp.

00:05:36.501 --> 00:05:38.868
[ Laughter ]

00:05:38.868 --> 00:05:41.067
They never, like, open the room,
and hear, like, click --

00:05:41.067 --> 00:05:42.400
"Surprised to see me?"

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:45.167
And it's like, "I can't see --
What? Hello?"

00:05:45.167 --> 00:05:48.267
"Oh, son of a gun.
Is this a lava lamp?"

00:05:48.267 --> 00:05:51.467
[ Laughter ]

00:05:51.467 --> 00:05:53.567
"Mm.

00:05:53.567 --> 00:05:56.033
Well, it's your ex-wife
who you thought was dead."

00:05:56.033 --> 00:05:57.501
[ Laughter ]

00:05:59.634 --> 00:06:02.334
Oh, you know, we did a surprise
inspection this week.

00:06:02.334 --> 00:06:06.334
And Ally Hord,
our wonderful writer --

00:06:06.334 --> 00:06:09.400
Ally Hord had a joke
about Watson and Crick

00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:11.367
and their discovery
of the double helix.

00:06:11.367 --> 00:06:14.267
Many of you pointed out it would
be a mistake to credit them

00:06:14.267 --> 00:06:19.501
and not also credit British
chemist Rosalind Franklin.

00:06:19.501 --> 00:06:21.534
So I reached out to Ally,
and I said, "You know,

00:06:21.534 --> 00:06:24.067
a lot of people think
Rosalind Franklin

00:06:24.067 --> 00:06:26.734
also deserves praise
for that discovery.

00:06:26.734 --> 00:06:30.434
And do you know -- do you know
what Ally said to me?

00:06:30.434 --> 00:06:33.300
"A woman scientist?
I don't think so."

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:35.434
[ Laughter ]

00:06:38.868 --> 00:06:40.834
[ Laughter ]

00:06:40.834 --> 00:06:42.267
It was my mistake.

00:06:42.267 --> 00:06:44.634
Ally -- Ally's joke did not
actually say the double helix

00:06:44.634 --> 00:06:46.033
when I was busting on it.

00:06:46.033 --> 00:06:51.067
And so it was -- I was the one
who erred there, not Ally.

00:06:51.067 --> 00:06:52.567
I did a -- I did James Corden.

00:06:52.567 --> 00:06:55.467
Our friend James Corden
had his last show --

00:06:55.467 --> 00:06:56.801
I guess, depending
on when you watch this,

00:06:56.801 --> 00:06:59.734
either today or --
or yesterday

00:06:59.734 --> 00:07:01.267
or three weeks from now.

00:07:01.267 --> 00:07:03.634
Is that how it works?
[ Laughs ]

00:07:03.634 --> 00:07:06.100
He had his final show,
and I did it last week.

00:07:06.100 --> 00:07:07.901
I'd never done it.
I had a wonderful time.

00:07:07.901 --> 00:07:11.234
And, you know, obviously, we --
you know, now sour grapes here,

00:07:11.234 --> 00:07:13.701
but "Corrections" has,
a couple of times now,

00:07:13.701 --> 00:07:16.767
lost to "Carpool Karaoke"
in its Emmy category.

00:07:16.767 --> 00:07:20.033
And so, you know,
I was there with him.

00:07:20.033 --> 00:07:21.267
I thought I'd ask
for a little bit of advice --

00:07:21.267 --> 00:07:22.501
you know,
"How can we break through?"

00:07:22.501 --> 00:07:24.033
And he said, you know,
"One of the nice things

00:07:24.033 --> 00:07:25.834
about 'Carpool Karaoke' is we
have, you know, the star power.

00:07:25.834 --> 00:07:28.000
We have guests who give it
a little bit of pop."

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:30.133
And I said,
"Oh, man, that would be great.

00:07:30.133 --> 00:07:33.334
Do you think, like,
Adele would do 'Corrections'?"

00:07:33.334 --> 00:07:35.868
And he said, "Yeah, you know,
you should ask her."

00:07:35.868 --> 00:07:37.467
And I said,
"Do you have her number?"

00:07:37.467 --> 00:07:40.000
And he was like, "Uh, yeah."

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:41.267
And I was like, "Great."

00:07:41.267 --> 00:07:42.601
He goes, "Do you have a pen?"
I was like, "Yep."

00:07:42.601 --> 00:07:45.834
And -- And then --
I didn't realize till I left

00:07:45.834 --> 00:07:48.234
because he goes,
"Here it is --

00:07:48.234 --> 00:07:52.801
1-877-K-A..."

00:07:52.801 --> 00:07:55.667
[ Laughter ]

00:08:00.434 --> 00:08:04.000
We talked about the Stevie Nicks
song "White Winged Dove."

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:08.367
Many of you had long thought
the lyric was "one-winged dove."

00:08:08.367 --> 00:08:10.334
I then pointed out
that a one-winged dove

00:08:10.334 --> 00:08:12.801
would just, you know,
sort of fly in circles.

00:08:12.801 --> 00:08:14.434
Um.

00:08:14.434 --> 00:08:16.434
A bunch of you pointed out
I was mistaken --

00:08:16.434 --> 00:08:19.567
I was mistaking what would
happen to a bird with one wing

00:08:19.567 --> 00:08:22.868
to what would happen to a boat
with one paddle

00:08:22.868 --> 00:08:25.334
because a boat with one paddle
would just go in circles,

00:08:25.334 --> 00:08:28.033
and a dove with one wing
would die real fast.

00:08:28.033 --> 00:08:30.334
[ Laughter ]

00:08:33.868 --> 00:08:35.267
Um.

00:08:35.267 --> 00:08:37.467
Jackal jokes.

00:08:37.467 --> 00:08:39.300
And every now and then,
the Jackals send in jokes.

00:08:39.300 --> 00:08:42.200
We were talking about, again,
this Tucker Carlson story,

00:08:42.200 --> 00:08:45.968
how Fox News has supposedly been
keeping an oppo file against him

00:08:45.968 --> 00:08:47.901
and how that
doesn't seem like a --

00:08:47.901 --> 00:08:49.734
I said, "I don't think
H.R. would approve

00:08:49.734 --> 00:08:55.601
if NBC kept an oppo file on me
and any of our staff."

00:08:55.601 --> 00:08:57.934
And...someone said

00:08:57.934 --> 00:09:01.968
it would be hard
for NBC H.R. to blackmail Wally

00:09:01.968 --> 00:09:05.133
because, when it comes to H.R.,
Wally is laying low.

00:09:05.133 --> 00:09:08.334
[ Laughter ]

00:09:12.434 --> 00:09:14.868
I didn't even get this one
right away.

00:09:14.868 --> 00:09:17.467
So, we talked about
Lindsey Graham's meemaw

00:09:17.467 --> 00:09:18.968
feeding shrimp to the chickens.

00:09:18.968 --> 00:09:20.868
And someone wrote --
And then we said,

00:09:20.868 --> 00:09:22.701
you know, because you wouldn't
feed shrimp to chickens.

00:09:22.701 --> 00:09:25.601
So someone wrote, "Chickens
eat any and everything,

00:09:25.601 --> 00:09:27.667
but never give them bok choy.

00:09:27.667 --> 00:09:29.400
I gave some
to our chickens one time

00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:31.701
and they still won't
shut up about it."

00:09:31.701 --> 00:09:34.234
[ Light laughter ]

00:09:34.234 --> 00:09:36.534
Do you get it, Shoemaker?

00:09:36.534 --> 00:09:38.067
Bok, bok, bok!

00:09:38.067 --> 00:09:40.100
[ Laughter ]

00:09:42.367 --> 00:09:44.133
I mean, you know,
it's a thinker,

00:09:44.133 --> 00:09:46.234
but, you know...

00:09:46.234 --> 00:09:48.534
it's structurally sound.

00:09:48.534 --> 00:09:49.834
[ Light laughter ]

00:09:51.133 --> 00:09:55.534
Someone else wrote,
"Do chickens ever eat anything

00:09:55.534 --> 00:09:58.501
and think,
'Mmm, tastes like me'?"

00:09:58.501 --> 00:10:01.801
[ Laughter ]

00:10:01.801 --> 00:10:03.868
I didn't -- These --
You can't be mad at me.

00:10:03.868 --> 00:10:06.167
[ Light laughter ]

00:10:06.167 --> 00:10:08.334
[ Clicking ]

00:10:09.067 --> 00:10:10.801
Um.

00:10:10.801 --> 00:10:13.868
We talked about a funeral coach

00:10:13.868 --> 00:10:15.767
and how I was on
the varsity funeral team in --

00:10:15.767 --> 00:10:17.300
in high school.

00:10:18.234 --> 00:10:19.834
It was a bit.

00:10:19.834 --> 00:10:22.367
[ Laughter ]

00:10:22.367 --> 00:10:25.834
But -- And hold on,
because this is...

00:10:27.467 --> 00:10:29.100
I'm going to get real.

00:10:29.100 --> 00:10:30.234
Someone said, "I don't believe

00:10:30.234 --> 00:10:31.868
Seth was ever
a varsity athlete."

00:10:31.868 --> 00:10:33.267
Well, I was.

00:10:33.267 --> 00:10:39.968
In fact, one year,
I lettered in track on our --

00:10:39.968 --> 00:10:43.133
Sorry. I lettered on our varsity
track team in high school.

00:10:43.133 --> 00:10:45.534
Track -- a cool thing about
track in my high school

00:10:45.534 --> 00:10:49.067
was the only sport
you couldn't get cut from.

00:10:49.067 --> 00:10:50.834
But this is --
Now, this is not a correction.

00:10:50.834 --> 00:10:52.601
This is just
a true story about --

00:10:52.601 --> 00:10:54.634
track was the only sport
I did in high school,

00:10:54.634 --> 00:10:57.200
but I did -- my junior year,

00:10:57.200 --> 00:11:00.634
I decided to go out
for the football team,

00:11:00.634 --> 00:11:02.400
and the reason I waited
until my junior year

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:04.300
is my brother was a freshman,

00:11:04.300 --> 00:11:07.434
and my brother decided to go out
for football as a freshman,

00:11:07.434 --> 00:11:10.133
which is the correct time
to start going out for a sport.

00:11:10.133 --> 00:11:11.334
And I don't -- it was --

00:11:11.334 --> 00:11:13.434
some sort of sibling-rivalry
thing kicked in

00:11:13.434 --> 00:11:15.400
because I never assumed
the Meyers boys

00:11:15.400 --> 00:11:17.033
were going to be
high-school football players.

00:11:17.033 --> 00:11:19.567
But as soon as my brother
decided he was going to do it,

00:11:19.567 --> 00:11:21.133
I just had to do it.

00:11:21.133 --> 00:11:24.434
And I knew the coach of
our high-school football team.

00:11:24.434 --> 00:11:26.567
His son and I
had grown up together.

00:11:26.567 --> 00:11:27.667
We were friends.

00:11:27.667 --> 00:11:29.167
And so I went and saw him,
and I said,

00:11:29.167 --> 00:11:30.868
"I want to go out
for the football team."

00:11:30.868 --> 00:11:32.067
And he said, "Don't --

00:11:32.067 --> 00:11:34.300
please don't come out
for the football team.

00:11:34.300 --> 00:11:35.634
You're never going to play.

00:11:35.634 --> 00:11:37.601
It's going to be a massive
waste of your time.

00:11:37.601 --> 00:11:39.534
I'm going to make you start
with the JV kids,

00:11:39.534 --> 00:11:41.634
so you're going to be spending
all your practices

00:11:41.634 --> 00:11:43.167
with freshman and sophomore."

00:11:43.167 --> 00:11:46.467
And I was like, "I don't care.
I'm going to take this on.

00:11:46.467 --> 00:11:48.434
I appreciate that I made
a mistake waiting,

00:11:48.434 --> 00:11:49.801
but this is very
important to me."

00:11:49.801 --> 00:11:52.067
And he said,
"Please don't do this.

00:11:52.067 --> 00:11:53.100
Please don't do this."

00:11:53.100 --> 00:11:54.434
And I said,
"I'm going to do it."

00:11:54.434 --> 00:11:55.968
And he goes,
"Well, I can't tell you not to."

00:11:55.968 --> 00:12:00.534
And so, that summer,
I did two-a-day practices --

00:12:00.534 --> 00:12:03.834
the worst time to participate
in football -- in August,

00:12:03.834 --> 00:12:05.467
and I did it
for the full two weeks.

00:12:05.467 --> 00:12:09.734
I should note my brother
made it two days before he quit.

00:12:09.734 --> 00:12:12.234
So the reason I was doing it
just walked out the door,

00:12:12.234 --> 00:12:14.300
and I was like,
"I'm going to stick with it,"

00:12:14.300 --> 00:12:17.534
because I knew, if I went home,
my dad would call me a quitter.

00:12:17.534 --> 00:12:19.534
But when your younger brother
goes home, they're like,

00:12:19.534 --> 00:12:21.033
"He's whimsical."

00:12:21.033 --> 00:12:23.367
[ Laughter ]

00:12:25.067 --> 00:12:28.067
Two weeks -- Two weeks
of two-a-day practices.

00:12:28.067 --> 00:12:30.300
Then it's the last day
of the summer,

00:12:30.300 --> 00:12:32.100
and I walk in,
I get on my full --

00:12:32.100 --> 00:12:33.200
This is a true story.

00:12:33.200 --> 00:12:34.501
This a very
traumatic story for me.

00:12:34.501 --> 00:12:36.033
I get in my full football gear.

00:12:36.033 --> 00:12:38.200
And then
the assistant coach says,

00:12:38.200 --> 00:12:39.367
"Coach wants to talk to you."

00:12:39.367 --> 00:12:40.801
And I go into
the coach's office,

00:12:40.801 --> 00:12:42.167
and he says, "I'm cutting you
from the football team."

00:12:42.167 --> 00:12:44.901
And I, for real, did not think
that was a possibility.

00:12:44.901 --> 00:12:46.400
And I said, "You're cutting me
from the football team?"

00:12:46.400 --> 00:12:48.033
He goes, "I just can't do this.

00:12:48.033 --> 00:12:50.601
I can't in good faith
have you waste all your time."

00:12:50.601 --> 00:12:52.801
He said, "I just feel like
you have other talents."

00:12:52.801 --> 00:12:54.934
And I said, "You -- I can't
believe you're doing that.

00:12:54.934 --> 00:12:56.033
I can't believe
you wasted two weeks."

00:12:56.033 --> 00:12:57.133
And he goes, "I do apologize.

00:12:57.133 --> 00:12:58.501
I should have
done it right away.

00:12:58.501 --> 00:13:00.100
You seemed so committed
to this idea,

00:13:00.100 --> 00:13:03.033
but I am cutting you
from the football team."

00:13:03.033 --> 00:13:05.734
And then I had to walk through
a locker room full of guys

00:13:05.734 --> 00:13:07.868
and then change
out of my football uniform

00:13:07.868 --> 00:13:09.734
and then walk out
in street clothes.

00:13:09.734 --> 00:13:13.100
And that's -- it's a significant
difference in outfits.

00:13:13.100 --> 00:13:16.133
Everyone know-- Even the dumbest
kid on the team was like,

00:13:16.133 --> 00:13:17.601
"Oh, something
just happened here."

00:13:17.601 --> 00:13:20.367
So...
[ Laughter ]

00:13:20.367 --> 00:13:21.968
And people
talked about it forever.

00:13:21.968 --> 00:13:24.267
No one had been cut
from the football team

00:13:24.267 --> 00:13:26.667
until I had been cut
from the football team,

00:13:26.667 --> 00:13:29.334
and everybody knew about it.

00:13:29.334 --> 00:13:31.033
They -- And they were friends.

00:13:31.033 --> 00:13:32.801
I had friends
on the football team.

00:13:32.801 --> 00:13:35.067
I then later -- you know,

00:13:35.067 --> 00:13:38.000
we had superlatives
in our high-school yearbook.

00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:40.367
I'm very happy to say I was
Best Sense of Humor

00:13:40.367 --> 00:13:42.934
in our high-school
superlatives.

00:13:42.934 --> 00:13:44.501
And I remember
a football player said,

00:13:44.501 --> 00:13:46.434
"You know, I voted for you."
And I said, "Thank you."

00:13:46.434 --> 00:13:48.100
And he said,
"I just thought it was so funny

00:13:48.100 --> 00:13:49.901
when you got cut
from the football team."

00:13:49.901 --> 00:13:52.901
[ Laughter ]

00:13:52.901 --> 00:13:55.801
Anyway,
I held a grudge for so long

00:13:55.801 --> 00:13:59.300
about the football coach
having put me through that

00:13:59.300 --> 00:14:02.801
because it was -- I mean, again,
as traumas go, it's nothing --

00:14:02.801 --> 00:14:05.100
but it really --
it really did stick with me

00:14:05.100 --> 00:14:06.300
for years and years.

00:14:06.300 --> 00:14:09.334
Anyway, you know,
15 years later, I'm on "SNL."

00:14:09.334 --> 00:14:14.400
I get asked to go back to my
high school to talk to the kids.

00:14:14.400 --> 00:14:16.167
And I'm walking
down the hallway,

00:14:16.167 --> 00:14:18.234
and the football coach
is now the guidance counselor.

00:14:18.234 --> 00:14:19.467
And I'm walking
down the hallway,

00:14:19.467 --> 00:14:20.934
and I'm still mad at him,

00:14:20.934 --> 00:14:22.300
and I see him
coming the other way.

00:14:22.300 --> 00:14:24.701
And he sees me,
and he just points at me.

00:14:24.701 --> 00:14:27.734
And he said, "I told you
you had other talents!"

00:14:27.734 --> 00:14:30.601
[ Laughter ]

00:14:30.601 --> 00:14:32.100
And I realized he'd been right
the whole time.

00:14:32.100 --> 00:14:33.934
I wasn't supposed
to play football.

00:14:33.934 --> 00:14:35.133
I was supposed to do
the other thing.

00:14:35.133 --> 00:14:36.968
I still iced him in the hallway.

00:14:36.968 --> 00:14:39.567
[ Laughter ]

00:14:39.567 --> 00:14:41.167
No, I know.

00:14:41.167 --> 00:14:46.601
Because, you guys, football --
my talents were not football.

00:14:46.601 --> 00:14:49.801
My talents are not
even a plural.

00:14:49.801 --> 00:14:52.167
[ Light laughter ]

00:14:52.167 --> 00:14:54.033
[ Laughter ]

00:14:54.033 --> 00:14:57.667
I'm good at one thing,
which is writing,

00:14:57.667 --> 00:15:00.133
and I love writing so much.

00:15:00.133 --> 00:15:01.834
I really do.

00:15:01.834 --> 00:15:04.834
And, you know,
I would do it for free.

00:15:04.834 --> 00:15:07.367
Some might say
writing "Corrections"

00:15:07.367 --> 00:15:08.834
is doing it for free.

00:15:08.834 --> 00:15:11.334
[ Laughter ]

00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:17.968
You might notice Pizza Hut's...

00:15:17.968 --> 00:15:21.501
[ Laughter ]

00:15:21.501 --> 00:15:23.701
...sponsorship ended at one.

00:15:23.701 --> 00:15:25.400
[ Laughter ]

00:15:26.534 --> 00:15:30.501
I am deeply proud of the fact

00:15:30.501 --> 00:15:32.367
that I get to be
a professional writer,

00:15:32.367 --> 00:15:38.601
and I bring this up because,
as of Monday at midnight,

00:15:38.601 --> 00:15:40.601
there might be
a writers' strike.

00:15:40.601 --> 00:15:43.767
And if a writers' strike
happens,

00:15:43.767 --> 00:15:47.267
that would shut down production
on a great many shows.

00:15:47.267 --> 00:15:52.501
And...I've been through this
before in 2007, 2008.

00:15:52.501 --> 00:15:54.400
There was a very long strike
when I was working at "SNL,"

00:15:54.400 --> 00:15:55.767
and it was really miserable.

00:15:55.767 --> 00:15:57.734
And it doesn't just affect
the writers,

00:15:57.734 --> 00:16:01.000
it affects all the incredible
non-writing staff

00:16:01.000 --> 00:16:02.334
on these shows.

00:16:02.334 --> 00:16:04.667
And it would really be
a miserable thing

00:16:04.667 --> 00:16:06.267
for people
to have to go through,

00:16:06.267 --> 00:16:11.734
especially considering we're on
the heels of that awful pandemic

00:16:11.734 --> 00:16:15.801
that affected obviously not just
show business, but all of us.

00:16:15.801 --> 00:16:20.534
And so I'm incredibly grateful
that there are negotiators

00:16:20.534 --> 00:16:22.901
for both sides
sitting and talking.

00:16:22.901 --> 00:16:29.367
I'm incredibly hopeful that
they can come to an agreement.

00:16:29.367 --> 00:16:33.300
I also feel very strongly that
what the writers are asking for

00:16:33.300 --> 00:16:35.100
is not unreasonable.

00:16:35.100 --> 00:16:39.234
And, as a proud member
of the Guild,

00:16:39.234 --> 00:16:42.200
I'm very grateful
that there is an organization

00:16:42.200 --> 00:16:47.501
that looks out for
the best interests of writers.

00:16:47.501 --> 00:16:51.901
So if you don't see me here
next week,

00:16:51.901 --> 00:16:58.000
know that it is something that
is not done lightly

00:16:58.000 --> 00:17:03.400
and that I will be heartbroken,
too, and miss you, as well.

00:17:03.400 --> 00:17:09.734
And let's just keep
our you-know-whats...

00:17:09.734 --> 00:17:14.400
and see me when you see me.

